# Registered test names that fail with PRISTINE C objects in this
# harness (kernel limits or environment), independent of any translation.
# swap-and-test.sh subtracts these from the -t filter so bundled
# always-broken tests cannot fail otherwise-correct translations, and
# passes every entry to test_progs -d (whole-name glob match, so a -t
# substring cannot pull a denied test back in). One entry per line, a test
# name or test/subtest; '#' comments.

# E2BIG / jump-sequence-too-complex at verification in the pinned kernel,
# both UML and QEMU flavors (checked with the unmodified C object).
verif_scale_pyperf600

# fs_kfuncs's set_remove_xattr subtest sets a security.bpf.* xattr from BPF
# and reads it back; the guest's root is the host filesystem over virtiofs,
# which has no security xattrs, so getxattr returns -1 for the pristine C
# object as well (test_fsverity, test_get_xattr, test_set_remove_xattr).
fs_kfuncs

# verifier_stack_arg exists only in a clang >= 23 build of the selftests
# (__BPF_FEATURE_STACK_ARGUMENT). At kernel pin 645f5fcf its subtest
# "stack_arg: pruning with different stack arg types" fails on the pristine
# C object: the verifier explores the branches in the other order, so the
# log says "arg JOIN insn 8 -> 10 ... _ + fp0-8" where the test expects
# "insn 9 -> 10 ... fp0-8 + _". The test is bundled into prog_tests/
# verifier.c, which consumes many translated objects.
verifier_stack_arg

# stack_arg_precision (clang >= 23 only, selected by substring from the
# stack_arg* translations): at pin 645f5fcf the pristine C object's
# "precision backtracking across bpf2bpf call for kfunc" subtest expects
# "mark_precise: frame0: parent state regs= stack=:  R1=1 R2=2 R3=3 R4=4
# R5=5 R10=fp0", which this verifier does not print.
stack_arg_precision/stack_arg: precision backtracking across bpf2bpf call for kfunc

# exceptions: with the clang 23 oracle, attaching exceptions_ext's
# `extension` to exceptions.bpf.o's global_subprog fails on the pristine C
# objects ("subprog() is not a global function": the kernel at 645f5fcf
# resolves the attach target to the static subprog of that object). Both
# subtests of this name go (the first, which expects -EINVAL, passes).
exceptions/non-throwing extension -> non-throwing subprog
# verifier_unpriv's first subtest is unprivileged-only; run alone (the gate
# runs only the tests that load the swapped object) libbpf probes
# FEAT_BTF_FUNC with the capabilities dropped, caches the failure for the
# process, and the next privileged program with a callback then loads
# without func_info ("missing btf func_info", -EINVAL) with the pristine C
# object as well. Running all of prog_tests/verifier.c hid this (an earlier
# privileged test warmed the cache): an upstream test-isolation bug.
verifier_unpriv/unpriv: pseudo function policy diagnostic

# arena_memcg: at pin 645f5fcf the pristine C object fails in this guest:
# the child that faults the 64M arena in under memory.max (cwd page cache
# over virtiofs as the reclaimable headroom) is killed by SIGBUS
# ("child faulted the arena in: got FALSE", memory.events max 25, oom 0),
# which is the failure mode the test attributes to a kernel without the
# arena memcg reclaim fix. The Rust object is the C's single map.
arena_memcg
